#!/usr/bin/env bash
source "$(dirname "$0")/common"

help() {
  cat << 'EOF'
ingest-colleges [-m <merit-aid.csv> -a <admission-factors.csv> -d <deadlines.csv>
                 -p <aid_policy.csv>]
                [-k <codebooks.json>] [-s <subjects.json>]
                [-v <money-vocabulary.json>]
                [-H <HD.csv> -I <IC.csv> -A <adm.csv> -C <C_A.csv> -y <YYYY>]
                [-S <sfa.csv> -f <YYYY>]
                <institution.csv> <fields.csv> [aliases.json]

Loads a version-pinned College Scorecard CSV pair into the `colleges` and
`college_programs` reference tables via the :college ingester (RFC 67). Upserts
on the natural keys, so re-running re-applies the same snapshot with no
duplicates. Malformed rows are logged and skipped — but a CSV missing a column
the loader reads is FATAL before any write (RFC 139).

The optional third argument is the curated college-aliases JSON (RFC 139),
defaulting to the repo's db/data/college-aliases.json. Each run ends with a
printed change summary and one college_index_build provenance row.

Every run also loads the generated published codebook (RFC 147) as its
`codebooks` phase, before the attribute phases: db/data/codebooks.json by
default, overridable with -k PATH (or an s3:// URL). The JVM
entry point REQUIRES it (migration 0067 made `colleges.state` and
`colleges.locale` foreign keys onto the codebook tables, so a run without one
can write no college row); this launcher is what supplies the path, because a
deployed binary has no repo layout to guess from. The load
upserts the ten reference domains, REFUSES to delete a row still referenced,
reports any drift in the recorded source digests, and — after the row phases —
reports every stored code with no codebook row (D46). Regenerate the file with
bin/fetch-codebooks; never hand-edit it.

Every run also loads the authored subject taxonomy (RFC 150) in its own
`subjects` phase, run immediately after `codebooks` so it is validated against
the CIP codes that phase just loaded:
db/data/subjects.json by default, overridable with -s PATH. Every
cip_prefix must match at least one real cip_codes row — a prefix that matches
none is FATAL and names the subject, because a subject that can never match a
college is indistinguishable, in a result, from one no college offers.

The taxonomy is MATERIALISED into college_search_index by the later
`search-index` phase, so editing db/data/subjects.json changes nothing that
search returns until this script runs again. The run prints the subject count
and the total expanded code count so a rebuild is visibly a rebuild.

The derived rebuilds run in this order at the end of a run: `name-words`, then
`canonical-money`, then `search-index`, then `provenance`. `canonical-money`
comes BEFORE `search-index` because the index sums price_figures into the
published on-campus price it ranks a family's search on (RFC 169); the reverse
order would index the PREVIOUS run's prices.

Every run also loads the authored money vocabulary (RFC 158) in its own
`money-vocabulary` phase after `subjects`: db/data/money-vocabulary.json by
default, overridable with -v PATH. The five vocabulary tables it fills are a
write precondition of the later `canonical-money` phase, which rebuilds
price_figures and cohort_money_stats wholesale from the institution CSV with
status-preserving readers (PrivacySuppressed survives as its own status). The
run logs the canonical row counts and the per-status breakdown on stderr, and
the `search-index` phase after it reads price_figures back.

The chat boundary reads that vocabulary ONCE, when rest-server and queue-worker
start. A codebook loaded into a live deployment therefore does NOT change what a
running service says until those two processes are restarted; the run prints a
reminder at the end of the codebooks summary.

With -m/-a/-d/-p, additionally loads the CDS seed (RFC 140, RFC 170) --
generated by bin/fetch-cds-seed, committed under db/seed/cds/ -- into
college_merit_aid, college_admission_factors and college_deadlines, plus the
canonical rows the aid-policy file carries (cohort_money_stats,
cohort_population_counts and aid_form_requirements), and prints the launch-set
coverage report. The four CDS options are all-or-nothing: pass all four or
none (the RFC 140 / brief 0004 D19 per-source-group shape). The
CDS loader asserts each file's header before writing and fatals on any
missing or renamed column. The CDS load is one phase of the same run and
commits BEFORE the provenance phase (RFC 148), so the college_index_build row
records the four seed digests and their row counts; a failed CDS load writes
no build row at all.

Run inside the nix dev shell (the JVM lives there):
  nix develop -c bin/ingest-colleges <institution.csv> <fields.csv>
  nix develop -c bin/ingest-colleges \
    -m db/seed/cds/merit-aid.csv \
    -a db/seed/cds/admission-factors.csv \
    -d db/seed/cds/deadlines.csv \
    -p db/seed/cds/aid_policy.csv \
    <institution.csv> <fields.csv>
IPEDS (RFC 144, extended by RFC 161; optional, ALL-OR-NOTHING). The six options
-H/-I/-A/-C/-Y/-y load the IPEDS institutional-characteristics and
published-charges surveys into `college_ipeds`, `college_programs_census` and
`college_ipeds_charges` in the SAME run. Give none of them and the run is
exactly as before; give any one and all six are required. -y is explicit
(e.g. 2023) and is stamped on every IPEDS row: it is never guessed from a
filename.

  Getting the files: run bin/fetch-ipeds. It downloads the archives,
  extracts the right member from each into db/seed/ipeds/ and records a
  PROVENANCE.json; pass the CSV paths it prints. Do not unzip them by hand — the
  member names are inconsistently cased (HD2023.csv, IC2023.csv, but adm2023.csv
  and C2023_a.csv) and IC/C_A also ship revised `_RV` members, and the fetcher is
  where that resolution lives. Only the NON-_RV member is ever loaded:
  IC2023_RV.csv is a reordered 141-column superset that a naive column check
  would accept and mis-map. The header assertion is the backstop, not the plan.
  The extracted CSVs are gitignored (bin/fetch-ipeds states why);
  db/seed/ipeds/PROVENANCE.json is what is committed. A successful fetch PRINTS
  the exact command below, with its own -y, so copy it from there.

Run inside the nix dev shell (the JVM lives there):
  nix develop -c bin/ingest-colleges <institution.csv> <fields.csv>
  nix develop -c bin/fetch-ipeds
  nix develop -c bin/ingest-colleges \
      -H db/seed/ipeds/HD2023.csv -I db/seed/ipeds/IC2023.csv \
      -A db/seed/ipeds/adm2023.csv -C db/seed/ipeds/C2023_a.csv \
      -Y db/seed/ipeds/ic2023_ay.csv -y 2023 \
      -S db/seed/ipeds/sfa2223.csv -f 2022 \
      inst.csv fields.csv aliases.json

IPEDS SFA (RFC 162, optional, ALL-OR-NOTHING, its own group). The two options
-S/-f load the IPEDS Student Financial Aid survey into `college_sfa` in the
SAME run, and the later `canonical-money` phase fills net price, Pell, the aid
mix and the population counts from it. -f is the START year of the file's AID
year (SFA2223 is aid year 2022-23, so -f 2022) and is a DIFFERENT number from
-y, the IPEDS collection year: SFA covers the year before the collection it
ships in, and one number cannot label both without lying about one. Both come
from bin/fetch-ipeds, which prints the whole command.

Options:
  -m FILE: CDS merit-aid seed CSV (Common Data Set)
  -a FILE: CDS admission-factors seed CSV (Common Data Set)
  -d FILE: CDS deadlines seed CSV (Common Data Set)
  -p FILE: CDS aid-policy seed CSV: the need figures and required forms (RFC 170)
  -k FILE: published codebook JSON (default: db/data/codebooks.json)
  -s FILE: authored subject taxonomy JSON (default: db/data/subjects.json)
  -v FILE: authored money vocabulary JSON (default: db/data/money-vocabulary.json)

  The IPEDS survey group (RFC 144, RFC 161) — all six together, or none of
  them. Every IPEDS flag is UPPER CASE except the year:
  -H FILE: IPEDS HD survey file, the institutional directory (HD2023.csv)
  -I FILE: IPEDS IC survey file, institutional characteristics (IC2023.csv)
  -A FILE: IPEDS ADM survey file, admissions and test scores (adm2023.csv)
  -C FILE: IPEDS completions survey file, by CIP code (C2023_a.csv)
  -Y FILE: IPEDS IC_AY survey file, published charges (ic2023_ay.csv)
  -y YYYY: IPEDS survey year, stamped on every IPEDS row (e.g. 2023)

  The IPEDS SFA group (RFC 162) — both together, or neither. A separate group
  from the six above because it is a separate survey on a separate year:
  -S FILE: IPEDS SFA survey file, student financial aid (sfa2223.csv)
  -f YYYY: SFA aid-year START year, stamped on every college_sfa row (e.g. 2022)

  Read the case pairs carefully. -a is the CDS admission-factors CSV and -A is
  the IPEDS ADM survey file; -Y is the IC_AY published-charges CSV and -y is the
  four-digit IPEDS collection year. Each pair is two different things, and
  getopts is case-sensitive, so a mistyped case is a different option, never a
  synonym. The SFA aid year is -f rather than a second case of -S or -Y for the
  same reason: -Y already names the IC_AY file (RFC 161), and -y already names
  the collection year, which is a DIFFERENT number from the aid year.

  -h: Help
EOF
  exit 0
}

CDS_MERIT_AID=""
CDS_ADMISSION_FACTORS=""
CDS_DEADLINES=""
CDS_AID_POLICY=""

# The IPEDS survey files (RFC 144). Parsed here (not just forwarded) because
# each file path must be run through resolve_file_arg before the JVM sees it,
# exactly as the three positionals are.
IPEDS_HD=""
IPEDS_IC=""
IPEDS_ADM=""
IPEDS_COMPLETIONS=""
IPEDS_IC_AY=""
IPEDS_SURVEY_YEAR=""
# The IPEDS SFA survey file and its aid year (RFC 162). Its OWN group, not part
# of the five above: a different survey on a different year, and joining them
# would break every existing five-flag invocation.
SFA_CSV=""
SFA_AID_YEAR=""
# The generated published codebook (RFC 147). Defaults to the repo copy, exactly
# as the aliases positional does; -k overrides it (and, like every other source
# arg, may be an s3:// URL). An explicitly-empty value is a wrong value, refused
# rather than silently replaced by the default.
CODEBOOKS_ARG=""
# The authored subject taxonomy (RFC 150). Same shape as -k above, and for the
# same reasons: a repo default, an s3:// source allowed, and an explicitly-empty
# value refused rather than silently replaced.
SUBJECTS_ARG=""
# The authored money vocabulary (RFC 158). Same shape as -s above, same reasons.
MONEY_VOCABULARY_ARG=""
# Every group flag seen, as "<group> -<flag>" entries in ONE array. The group
# travels in the VALUE, not in the name of a per-group array, so the three rules
# below have exactly one implementation each however many groups this script
# grows -- and a fix to one group cannot leave another accepting what it should
# refuse.
GROUP_GIVEN=()

# Presence is judged on the FLAG, not on its value, and each flag may appear
# once — the two rules IngestApplication's own grammar states. Judging presence
# on the value would make six EMPTY flags look like an omitted group and
# silently downgrade the run to Scorecard-only with a green exit; keeping only
# the last of a repeated flag would silently discard a file the JVM calls a
# usage error. Both are refused here, before any download.
set_group_flag() {
  local group="$1" flag="$2" value="$3" seen
  for seen in ${GROUP_GIVEN[@]+"${GROUP_GIVEN[@]}"}; do
    [ "$seen" = "$group -$flag" ] &&
      fatal -s "$EXIT_INVALID_ARG_VALUE" "ingest-colleges: [-$flag] was given more than once"
  done
  [ -n "$value" ] ||
    fatal -s "$EXIT_INVALID_ARG_VALUE" "ingest-colleges: [-$flag] must have a non-empty value"
  GROUP_GIVEN+=("$group -$flag")
}

# The flags of group $1 that were given, space separated, in the order they were
# given. Empty means the group is absent -- which is what the argv builders below
# test, so "was this group passed at all" is asked in one way.
list_group_flags() {
  local group="$1" seen out=""
  for seen in ${GROUP_GIVEN[@]+"${GROUP_GIVEN[@]}"}; do
    case "$seen" in
    "$group "*) out="$out ${seen#"$group "}" ;;
    esac
  done
  printf '%s' "${out# }"
}

# A group is ALL-OR-NOTHING: given its name in $1 and every flag it requires in
# $2.., either all were given or none was. A partial group is a usage error,
# never a silent partial load. Refused here as well as in the JVM so the failure
# costs no download and no JVM start; the JVM keeps its own copy of this rule
# because it is reachable without this script.
require_group_complete() {
  local group="$1" given missing="" flag
  shift
  given="$(list_group_flags "$group")"
  [ -n "$given" ] || return 0
  for flag in "$@"; do
    case " $given " in
    *" -$flag "*) ;;
    *) missing="$missing -$flag" ;;
    esac
  done
  [ -n "$missing" ] || return 0
  fatal -s "$EXIT_MISSING_REQUIRED_ARG" "ingest-colleges: the [$group] options are all-or-nothing; given [$given], also required: [${missing# }]"
}

# An empty CDS option value is a usage error, not an omission: the all-or-nothing
# group below counts non-empty values, so an empty one would silently drop the
# whole group.
require_cds_value() {
  [ -n "$2" ] || fatal -s "$EXIT_INVALID_ARG_VALUE" "ingest-colleges: [-$1] must have a non-empty value"
}

# ONE getopts loop parses the whole grammar (bin/ conventions: short options
# only, no hand-rolled argument loop). The IPEDS group is upper case throughout
# — -H/-I/-A/-C/-Y plus the -y year — so -a (the CDS admission-factors CSV) and
# -A (the IPEDS ADM survey file) stay distinct, as do -Y (the IC_AY charges CSV)
# and -y (the survey year); the SFA group adds -S (its CSV) and -f (its aid
# year). The help text spells all of them out.
while getopts ":m:a:d:p:k:s:v:H:I:A:C:Y:y:S:f:h" opt; do
  case "$opt" in
  # An EMPTY value is a wrong value, never an omitted option: without these
  # guards `-p ""` counts as "the CDS group was not passed" and the whole phase
  # is skipped SILENTLY, which is the sentinel -k/-s/-v already refuse.
  m) CDS_MERIT_AID="$OPTARG";           require_cds_value m "$CDS_MERIT_AID" ;;
  a) CDS_ADMISSION_FACTORS="$OPTARG";   require_cds_value a "$CDS_ADMISSION_FACTORS" ;;
  d) CDS_DEADLINES="$OPTARG";           require_cds_value d "$CDS_DEADLINES" ;;
  p) CDS_AID_POLICY="$OPTARG";          require_cds_value p "$CDS_AID_POLICY" ;;
  k) CODEBOOKS_ARG="$OPTARG"
     [ -n "$CODEBOOKS_ARG" ] ||
       fatal -s "$EXIT_INVALID_ARG_VALUE" "ingest-colleges: [-k] must have a non-empty value"
     ;;
  s) SUBJECTS_ARG="$OPTARG"
     [ -n "$SUBJECTS_ARG" ] ||
       fatal -s "$EXIT_INVALID_ARG_VALUE" "ingest-colleges: [-s] must have a non-empty value"
     ;;
  v) MONEY_VOCABULARY_ARG="$OPTARG"
     [ -n "$MONEY_VOCABULARY_ARG" ] ||
       fatal -s "$EXIT_INVALID_ARG_VALUE" "ingest-colleges: [-v] must have a non-empty value"
     ;;
  H) IPEDS_HD="$OPTARG";          set_group_flag IPEDS H "$IPEDS_HD" ;;
  I) IPEDS_IC="$OPTARG";          set_group_flag IPEDS I "$IPEDS_IC" ;;
  A) IPEDS_ADM="$OPTARG";         set_group_flag IPEDS A "$IPEDS_ADM" ;;
  C) IPEDS_COMPLETIONS="$OPTARG"; set_group_flag IPEDS C "$IPEDS_COMPLETIONS" ;;
  Y) IPEDS_IC_AY="$OPTARG";       set_group_flag IPEDS Y "$IPEDS_IC_AY" ;;
  y) IPEDS_SURVEY_YEAR="$OPTARG"; set_group_flag IPEDS y "$IPEDS_SURVEY_YEAR" ;;
  S) SFA_CSV="$OPTARG";           set_group_flag SFA S "$SFA_CSV" ;;
  f) SFA_AID_YEAR="$OPTARG";      set_group_flag SFA f "$SFA_AID_YEAR" ;;
  h) help ;;
  \?) fatal -s "$EXIT_UNKNOWN_OPTION" "unknown option [-$OPTARG]" ;;
  :)  fatal -s "$EXIT_OPTION_REQUIRES_VALUE" "option [-$OPTARG] requires an argument" ;;
  esac
done
shift $((OPTIND-1))

# getopts stops at the first operand, so an option written AFTER the positionals
# is left in "$@" and would otherwise be read as a file name — a retired long
# option such as --hd=HD.csv would silently become the aliases argument. Nothing
# this script takes positionally begins with "-", so anything that does is an
# option in the wrong place or an option this script does not have.
for candidate in "$@"; do
  case "$candidate" in
  -*) fatal -s "$EXIT_UNKNOWN_OPTION" \
    "ingest-colleges: unknown option [$candidate]; options come before <institution.csv>" ;;
  esac
done

if [ "$#" -lt 2 ] || [ "$#" -gt 3 ]; then
  fatal -s "$EXIT_MISSING_REQUIRED_ARG" "ingest-colleges requires two or three arguments: <institution.csv> <fields.csv> [aliases.json]"
fi

# An explicitly-passed EMPTY third argument is a wrong value, not an omitted
# one: `${3-...}` (not `${3:-...}`) keeps the two cases distinct, and the empty
# one is refused rather than silently ingesting the repo default. Checked with
# the other grammar guards, before any download or resolve work.
if [ "$#" -eq 3 ] && [ -z "$3" ]; then
  fatal -s "$EXIT_MISSING_REQUIRED_ARG" "ingest-colleges: the aliases argument, when given, must not be empty"
fi

# The CDS seed options are all-or-nothing (one source group, RFC 140): a partial
# set would load one table's cycle without its siblings and skew the coverage
# report, so it is rejected up front.
CDS_OPTION_COUNT=0
for cds_arg in "$CDS_MERIT_AID" "$CDS_ADMISSION_FACTORS" "$CDS_DEADLINES" "$CDS_AID_POLICY"; do
  [ -n "$cds_arg" ] && CDS_OPTION_COUNT=$((CDS_OPTION_COUNT + 1))
done
if [ "$CDS_OPTION_COUNT" -ne 0 ] && [ "$CDS_OPTION_COUNT" -ne 4 ]; then
  fatal -s "$EXIT_MISSING_REQUIRED_ARG" "the CDS options are all-or-nothing: pass -m, -a, -d and -p together, or none"
fi

# The IPEDS group (RFC 144, gate-2 D19, extended by RFC 161) and the SFA group
# (RFC 162) are each all-or-nothing. SFA is its own group -- a different survey
# on a different year -- so a Scorecard+IPEDS run that predates RFC 162 keeps
# working unchanged: a file with no aid year would have to guess the year from a
# filename, and a year with no file loads nothing.
require_group_complete IPEDS H I A C Y y
require_group_complete SFA S f

# Own the scratch dir resolve_file_arg downloads s3:// args into: the file
# must outlive that (subshell) call, so this script holds the EXIT trap.
INGEST_SCRATCH="$(mktemp -d)"
trap 'rm -rf "$INGEST_SCRATCH"' EXIT

# Resolve each CSV arg to an absolute path: local (realpath) or s3://
# (downloaded into INGEST_SCRATCH), so the same script serves a developer's
# local run and a bin/remote-triggered run.
INSTITUTION_CSV="$(resolve_file_arg "$1" "$INGEST_SCRATCH")"
FIELDS_CSV="$(resolve_file_arg "$2" "$INGEST_SCRATCH")"

# The curated aliases file (RFC 139): optional third arg, defaulting to the
# repo-committed seed. Resolved like the CSVs so an s3:// alias file also works.
ALIASES_ARG="${3-$PROJECT_ROOT/db/data/college-aliases.json}"
ALIASES_JSON="$(resolve_file_arg "$ALIASES_ARG" "$INGEST_SCRATCH")"

# The published codebook (RFC 147), loaded by the run's `codebooks` phase before
# the attribute phases. Resolved like every other source, and its ORIGINAL
# argument forwarded as --codebooks-source so the provenance row records what the
# caller named.
CODEBOOKS_ARG="${CODEBOOKS_ARG:-$PROJECT_ROOT/db/data/codebooks.json}"
CODEBOOKS_JSON="$(resolve_file_arg "$CODEBOOKS_ARG" "$INGEST_SCRATCH")"

# The authored subject taxonomy (RFC 150), loaded by its own `subjects` phase,
# run immediately after `codebooks` so it is validated against the CIP codes
# that phase just loaded. Resolved and forwarded exactly like the codebook above.
SUBJECTS_ARG="${SUBJECTS_ARG:-$PROJECT_ROOT/db/data/subjects.json}"
SUBJECTS_JSON="$(resolve_file_arg "$SUBJECTS_ARG" "$INGEST_SCRATCH")"

# The authored money vocabulary (RFC 158), loaded by its own `money-vocabulary`
# phase after `subjects` -- the five reference tables the `canonical-money`
# phase foreign-keys into. Resolved and forwarded exactly like the taxonomy.
MONEY_VOCABULARY_ARG="${MONEY_VOCABULARY_ARG:-$PROJECT_ROOT/db/data/money-vocabulary.json}"
MONEY_VOCABULARY_JSON="$(resolve_file_arg "$MONEY_VOCABULARY_ARG" "$INGEST_SCRATCH")"

# Pass the CDS seed paths to the launcher as NAMED flags, so the file-to-table
# mapping is explicit on the command line (and in `ps` output) instead of being
# a positional convention two files have to keep in step.
CDS_ARGS=()
if [ "$CDS_OPTION_COUNT" -eq 4 ]; then
  CDS_ARGS+=(--cds-merit "$(resolve_file_arg "$CDS_MERIT_AID" "$INGEST_SCRATCH")")
  CDS_ARGS+=(--cds-factors "$(resolve_file_arg "$CDS_ADMISSION_FACTORS" "$INGEST_SCRATCH")")
  CDS_ARGS+=(--cds-deadlines "$(resolve_file_arg "$CDS_DEADLINES" "$INGEST_SCRATCH")")
  CDS_ARGS+=(--cds-aid-policy "$(resolve_file_arg "$CDS_AID_POLICY" "$INGEST_SCRATCH")")
fi

# The IPEDS files get the same treatment, and the ORIGINAL argument of each is
# forwarded as its --*-source partner so the provenance row records what the
# caller actually named (RFC 144).
IPEDS_ARGV=()
if [ -n "$(list_group_flags IPEDS)" ]; then
  HD_CSV="$(resolve_file_arg "$IPEDS_HD" "$INGEST_SCRATCH")"
  IC_CSV="$(resolve_file_arg "$IPEDS_IC" "$INGEST_SCRATCH")"
  ADM_CSV="$(resolve_file_arg "$IPEDS_ADM" "$INGEST_SCRATCH")"
  COMPLETIONS_CSV="$(resolve_file_arg "$IPEDS_COMPLETIONS" "$INGEST_SCRATCH")"
  IC_AY_CSV="$(resolve_file_arg "$IPEDS_IC_AY" "$INGEST_SCRATCH")"
  IPEDS_ARGV=(
    --hd="$HD_CSV" --ic="$IC_CSV" --adm="$ADM_CSV" --completions="$COMPLETIONS_CSV"
    --ic-ay="$IC_AY_CSV"
    --survey-year="$IPEDS_SURVEY_YEAR"
    --hd-source="$IPEDS_HD" --ic-source="$IPEDS_IC" --adm-source="$IPEDS_ADM"
    --completions-source="$IPEDS_COMPLETIONS" --ic-ay-source="$IPEDS_IC_AY"
  )
fi

# The SFA file gets the same treatment as the IPEDS five: resolved, and its
# ORIGINAL argument forwarded as --sfa-source so the provenance row records what
# the caller named (RFC 162).
SFA_ARGV=()
if [ -n "$(list_group_flags SFA)" ]; then
  SFA_RESOLVED="$(resolve_file_arg "$SFA_CSV" "$INGEST_SCRATCH")"
  SFA_ARGV=(
    --sfa="$SFA_RESOLVED" --sfa-aid-year="$SFA_AID_YEAR" --sfa-source="$SFA_CSV"
  )
fi

# Run the prebuilt installDist launcher directly (never Gradle), fataling if the
# build artifact is absent (bin/INVARIANTS.md pre-built-launcher rule). The path is
# a single $PROJECT_ROOT-relative path that resolves identically in the local dev
# tree and under /opt/unicoach/current (bin/deploy tars the college dist preserving
# its build/install path, and bin/remote runs the tool from /opt/unicoach/current).
# IngestApplication takes the three resolved paths as positional argv, followed by
# the provenance flags (RFC 139) carrying each source's ORIGINAL argument (local
# path or s3:// URL) — the college_index_build row records these so a run is
# traceable to its real source — and then, when the CDS group is passed, the
# four seed paths behind --cds-merit/--cds-factors/--cds-deadlines/--cds-aid-policy
# (RFC 140, RFC 170),
# so the file-to-table mapping is explicit in argv instead of positional.
# COLLEGE_DIST (bin/functions) is the single source of truth for the dist path,
# shared with bin/deploy.
LAUNCHER="$PROJECT_ROOT/$COLLEGE_DIST/bin/college"
[ -x "$LAUNCHER" ] || fatal "college launcher not found at [$LAUNCHER]; run bin/build-college first"
# CDS_ARGS is expanded as `${ARR[@]+"${ARR[@]}"}`, never the obvious
# `"${CDS_ARGS[@]}"`: it is EMPTY on the ordinary Scorecard-only run, and
# expanding an empty array is an unbound-variable error under bash 3.2 +
# `set -u` (see split_argv_at_dashdash in bin/functions).
"$LAUNCHER" "$INSTITUTION_CSV" "$FIELDS_CSV" "$ALIASES_JSON" \
  --institution-source="$1" --fields-source="$2" --aliases-source="$ALIASES_ARG" \
  --codebooks="$CODEBOOKS_JSON" --codebooks-source="$CODEBOOKS_ARG" \
  --subjects="$SUBJECTS_JSON" --subjects-source="$SUBJECTS_ARG" \
  --money-vocabulary="$MONEY_VOCABULARY_JSON" --money-vocabulary-source="$MONEY_VOCABULARY_ARG" \
  ${CDS_ARGS[@]+"${CDS_ARGS[@]}"} \
  ${IPEDS_ARGV[@]+"${IPEDS_ARGV[@]}"} \
  ${SFA_ARGV[@]+"${SFA_ARGV[@]}"}
